<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Languages and Their Peoples</h1><p class="path-tagline">Ten languages and the peoples, cities and empires that spoke, wrote and carried them.</p><p class="path-intro">A language travels with the people who speak it and the states that use it. This path follows ten of them, roughly in order of age, from Sumerian, first written at Uruk around 3200 BCE, to Swahili, which grew in the merchant towns of the East African coast. Some, such as Sumerian, Latin and Ge'ez, outlived their speakers as written languages of learning, law or worship. Others, such as Arabic, Nahuatl and Swahili, became common languages of faith, empire or trade far beyond the lands where they began.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/sumerian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 3200 BCE – c. 80 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Sumerian</span><span class="path-step-why">The language of the city-states of southern Mesopotamia, whose speakers developed cuneiform around 3200 BCE; it stayed a language of learning long after it ceased to be spoken.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Sumerian language was the language of ancient Sumer, one of the earliest written languages.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/akkadian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 2500 BCE – c. 80 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Akkadian</span><span class="path-step-why">The oldest recorded Semitic language, spoken in the empires of Akkad, Babylon and Assyria, and in the Amarna letters the language of diplomacy between Egypt and its neighbours.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Akkadian is an extinct Semitic language of ancient Mesopotamia, written in cuneiform.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/sanskrit"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 1500 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Sanskrit</span><span class="path-step-why">First known from the hymns of the Rigveda, composed in the north-west of the subcontinent around 1500–1200 BCE, and codified by Panini in some four thousand rules.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Sanskrit is a classical Indo-Aryan language of South Asia, used for religious, literary, philosophical and scientific texts over more than three millennia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/classical-chinese"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · c. 1250 BCE – c. 1920 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Classical Chinese</span><span class="path-step-why">The written language of Confucius and the historians of ancient China, rooted in Shang oracle bones and used for government in Korea, Japan and Vietnam.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/latin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-forum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 600 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Latin</span><span class="path-step-why">The language of Latium and Rome, which spread with Roman power and changed region by region into Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Latin is the language of ancient Rome, which spread across the western Mediterranean with Roman rule and became the root of the Romance languages.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/arabic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · c. 100 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Arabic</span><span class="path-step-why">First recorded in inscriptions of the Arabian peninsula and its desert borderlands, it became a world language with the Qur'an and the conquests of the 7th century.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Arabic is a Semitic language that spread with Islam from the Arabian Peninsula across the Middle East and North Africa.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/geez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · c. 300 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Ge'ez</span><span class="path-step-why">The language of the Kingdom of Aksum, carved on stone from the 4th century, and still the liturgical language of the Ethiopian and Eritrean churches and the Beta Israel.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Ge'ez is an ancient Semitic language of Ethiopia and Eritrea, with its own script.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/old-english"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beowulf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · c. 450 CE – c. 1150 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Old English</span><span class="path-step-why">Grown from the dialects of the Angles, Saxons and Jutes who settled in Britain, it became a language of government under Alfred the Great until the Norman Conquest.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/nahuatl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 500 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Nahuatl</span><span class="path-step-why">The language of the Aztecs and of trade and empire across much of Mesoamerica, written after the Spanish conquest in the Latin alphabet, as in the Florentine Codex.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Nahuatl is a Uto-Aztecan language spoken by the Mexica and other peoples of central Mexico.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/language/swahili"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · c. 800 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Swahili</span><span class="path-step-why">The Bantu speech of the East African merchant towns, rich in Arabic words from Indian Ocean trade, which caravans carried inland from Zanzibar in the 19th century.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Swahili are a people of the East African coast, whose culture developed through interaction between Bantu-speaking Africans and Arab and Persian traders.</span></span></a></li></ol>
